  "worked_solution": "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(8,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-8)^{2}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{9x+1}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-8)^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-8)^{2}+(\\sqrt{9x+1})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-8)^{2}+9x+1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-16x+64+9x+1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-7x+65.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-7 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =7 \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=\\frac{7}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=\\frac{7}{2}.$",
